{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/client/responseCache.ts | sed -n '52,112p;246,276p;405,425p;540,568p'; nl -ba src/client/client.ts | sed -n '1680,1718p'; nl -ba test/client/responseCache.test.ts | sed -n '150,180p'; nl -ba ../../docs/clients/caching.md | sed -n '58,72p'\"","aggregated_output":"    52\t}\n    53\t\n    54\t/**\n    55\t * One cached response body. Values written by the client are serialized JSON\n    56\t * protocol documents rather than live result objects. The `unknown` type is\n    57\t * retained for source compatibility with existing custom stores; the client\n    58\t * can also read their legacy decoded-object entries during migration.\n    59\t * `stamp` is the store-generated monotonically increasing write counter —\n    60\t * opaque to callers. Derived views (e.g. a `name → Tool` index) memoize\n    61\t * against it and re-derive only when it changes. `expiresAt` (absolute ms\n    62\t * epoch, `now + ttlMs`) and `scope` are the client-computed freshness metadata; the store\n    63\t * MUST persist them and hand them back on `get` so the read path can decide\n    64\t * freshness and gate the shared-partition fallback on `scope === 'public'`.\n    65\t */\n    66\texport interface CacheEntry {\n    67\t    readonly value: unknown;\n    68\t    readonly stamp: number;\n    69\t    readonly expiresAt?: number;\n    70\t    readonly scope?: CacheScope;\n    71\t}\n    72\t\n    73\t/**\n    74\t * The pluggable response-cache store. The interface is intentionally narrow;\n    75\t * the in-memory default is the only implementation the SDK ships.\n    76\t *\n    77\t * Every method is async-ready ({@linkcode MaybePromise}) so a Redis-style\n    78\t * store can implement the same interface without a later breaking change; the\n    79\t * in-memory default stays synchronous (plain values are valid under\n    80\t * `MaybePromise`). The `Client` `await`s every call site.\n    81\t *\n    82\t * Entries are keyed by `{method, params, partition}` where `partition` is the\n    83\t * `Client`-derived `[serverIdentity, principal]` JSON pair, so one store\n    84\t * instance is safe to share across `Client` instances connected to different\n    85\t * servers and/or principals: writes from distinct connections never collide,\n    86\t * the shared-partition read fallback is gated on the stored\n    87\t * `scope === 'public'`, and `list_changed` / `HEADER_MISMATCH` evictions are\n    88\t * scoped to the connected server's two partitions — co-tenants on a shared\n    89\t * store are unaffected. The `Client` constructor still allocates a fresh\n    90\t * {@linkcode InMemoryResponseCacheStore} per instance by default; supply your\n    91\t * own to share or persist.\n    92\t */\n    93\texport interface ResponseCacheStore {\n    94\t    get(key: CacheKey): MaybePromise<CacheEntry | undefined>;\n    95\t    /**\n    96\t     * Writes `entry` under `key` and returns the store-generated stamp the\n    97\t     * resulting {@linkcode CacheEntry} carries. The store owns the stamp\n    98\t     * counter; callers do not supply one. The caller owns `expiresAt` and\n    99\t     * `scope` (the client-computed freshness metadata); the store MUST persist\n   100\t     * them and hand them back on `get`. Client writes carry `value` as a\n   101\t     * serialized JSON protocol document.\n   102\t     */\n   103\t    set(key: CacheKey, entry: { value: unknown; expiresAt?: number; scope?: CacheScope }): MaybePromise<number>;\n   104\t    /**\n   105\t     * Drop the single entry under `key` (no-op if absent). Called for both\n   106\t     * `notifications/resources/updated` (per-URI) and the `list_changed`\n   107\t     * notifications (the list singletons live at `{method, params: '', partition}`).\n   108\t     */\n   109\t    delete(key: CacheKey): MaybePromise<void>;\n   110\t    /**\n   111\t     * Drop every entry for `method` across every partition. The `Client` does\n   112\t     * NOT call this (its `list_changed` path issues two partition-scoped\n   246\tfunction genKey(method: string, params?: string): string {\n   247\t    return params === undefined ? method : `${method}\\0${params}`;\n   248\t}\n   249\t\n   250\t/**\n   251\t * Encode a decoded protocol result for storage. JSON is the cache boundary:\n   252\t * stores receive a portable document rather than references to SDK runtime\n   253\t * objects, and parsing it later supplies the defensive copy needed on hits.\n   254\t */\n   255\texport function serializeCacheDocument(value: unknown): string {\n   256\t    const document = JSON.stringify(value);\n   257\t    if (document === undefined) throw new TypeError('Cache values must be JSON-serializable protocol documents');\n   258\t    return document;\n   259\t}\n   260\t\n   261\t/**\n   262\t * Decode a stored protocol document. Non-string values are accepted as a\n   263\t * compatibility bridge for custom stores populated by older SDK releases;\n   264\t * routing those values through JSON still gives them the same protocol-\n   265\t * document semantics and mutation isolation as newly written entries.\n   266\t */\n   267\texport function deserializeCacheDocument(value: unknown): unknown {\n   268\t    return JSON.parse(typeof value === 'string' ? value : JSON.stringify(value));\n   269\t}\n   270\t\n   271\t/** Decode an entry without exposing the store-owned metadata object. */\n   272\tfunction deserializeCacheEntry(entry: CacheEntry): CacheEntry {\n   273\t    return { ...entry, value: deserializeCacheDocument(entry.value) };\n   274\t}\n   275\t\n   276\t/**\n   405\t     * on `entry.scope === 'public'` — a co-tenant client that omits\n   406\t     * `cachePartition` writes its `'private'`-scoped entries at the public\n   407\t     * partition, and serving those to a correctly-partitioned client would\n   408\t     * leak private bodies (mcp.d's `cachedEntry` two-probe order; the scope\n   409\t     * gate is defence-in-depth on top of the partition split). When\n   410\t     * `cachePartition` is `''` the two partitions are identical and only one\n   411\t     * probe is issued.\n   412\t     */\n   413\t    private async _probe(method: string, params?: string): Promise<CacheEntry | undefined> {\n   414\t        const key = { method, params: params ?? '' };\n   415\t        const ownPartition = this._partitionFor('private');\n   416\t        const own = await this._store.get({ ...key, partition: ownPartition });\n   417\t        if (own !== undefined) return deserializeCacheEntry(own);\n   418\t        const sharedPartition = this._partitionFor('public');\n   419\t        if (sharedPartition === ownPartition) return undefined;\n   420\t        const shared = await this._store.get({ ...key, partition: sharedPartition });\n   421\t        return shared?.scope === 'public' ? deserializeCacheEntry(shared) : undefined;\n   422\t    }\n   423\t\n   424\t    /**\n   425\t     * Bump the per-method generation (so an in-flight {@linkcode write} for the\n   540\t     * is deleted from the OPPOSITE partition (mirroring {@linkcode evictKey}'s\n   541\t     * two-partition posture). A server that flips a result's `cacheScope` for\n   542\t     * the same key would otherwise leave the previous entry in the other slot\n   543\t     * — and since `_probe` checks own-partition first, a stale private entry\n   544\t     * would shadow the fresh public one (or a stale public entry would keep\n   545\t     * serving co-tenants). Both store calls are independently guarded so a\n   546\t     * custom store's failure on one does not skip the other.\n   547\t     */\n   548\t    async write(\n   549\t        method: string,\n   550\t        value: unknown,\n   551\t        capturedGen: number,\n   552\t        freshness?: { expiresAt: number; scope: CacheScope; params?: string }\n   553\t    ): Promise<void> {\n   554\t        if ((this._evictionGeneration.get(genKey(method, freshness?.params)) ?? 0) !== capturedGen) return;\n   555\t        const params = freshness?.params ?? '';\n   556\t        const ownPartition = this._partitionFor('private');\n   557\t        const sharedPartition = this._partitionFor('public');\n   558\t        const partition = (freshness?.scope ?? 'private') === 'public' ? sharedPartition : ownPartition;\n   559\t        try {\n   560\t            await this._store.set(\n   561\t                { method, params, partition },\n   562\t                { value: serializeCacheDocument(value), expiresAt: freshness?.expiresAt, scope: freshness?.scope }\n   563\t            );\n   564\t        } catch (error) {\n   565\t            this._reportError(error);\n   566\t        }\n   567\t        if (sharedPartition !== ownPartition) {\n   568\t            try {\n  1680\t    }\n  1681\t\n  1682\t    /**\n  1683\t     * The cache-serving front of every cacheable verb (mcp.d's `cachedFetch`\n  1684\t     * read half): under `cacheMode: 'use'` (the default), a held entry whose\n  1685\t     * `expiresAt` is in the future is returned from a freshly decoded cache\n  1686\t     * document and the round trip is skipped. `'refresh'` and `'bypass'` always fetch (the\n  1687\t     * caller decides whether to write). A custom store whose `get()` rejects\n  1688\t     * is routed to `onerror` and treated as a miss — cache bookkeeping never\n  1689\t     * blocks a request from reaching the wire.\n  1690\t     */\n  1691\t    private async _serveFromCache<R>(\n  1692\t        method: string,\n  1693\t        params: string | undefined,\n  1694\t        options: CacheableRequestOptions | undefined\n  1695\t    ): Promise<R | undefined> {\n  1696\t        if (options?.cacheMode === 'bypass' || options?.cacheMode === 'refresh') return undefined;\n  1697\t        const entry = await this._cache.read(method, params).catch(error => void this._reportStoreError(error));\n  1698\t        if (entry?.expiresAt !== undefined && entry.expiresAt > this._cache.now()) {\n  1699\t            // A pre-aborted caller signal must reject the same way it would on\n  1700\t            // the wire path (`Protocol.request()` wraps an already-aborted\n  1701\t            // signal as `SdkError(RequestTimeout, reason)`); without this guard\n  1702\t            // a cache hit would resolve successfully and silently swallow the\n  1703\t            // abort.\n  1704\t            if (options?.signal?.aborted) {\n  1705\t                const reason = options.signal.reason;\n  1706\t                throw reason instanceof SdkError ? reason : new SdkError(SdkErrorCode.RequestTimeout, String(reason));\n  1707\t            }\n  1708\t            // ClientResponseCache decodes the stored JSON document on every\n  1709\t            // read, so this value is already a fresh object. A caller can\n  1710\t            // mutate it without reaching the stored document or the\n  1711\t            // stamp-memoized indices derived from another decode.\n  1712\t            return entry.value as R;\n  1713\t        }\n  1714\t        return undefined;\n  1715\t    }\n  1716\t\n  1717\t    /** Route a custom-store failure to `onerror` without aborting the surrounding dispatch. */\n  1718\t    private _reportStoreError(e: unknown): void {\n   150\t        expect(store.get({ method: 'resources/read', params: 'b', partition: 'p' })?.value).toBe(2);\n   151\t        // Absent key: no-op.\n   152\t        store.delete({ method: 'resources/read', params: 'a', partition: 'p' });\n   153\t    });\n   154\t});\n   155\t\n   156\tdescribe('ClientResponseCache', () => {\n   157\t    it('cache document codec round-trips JSON values into detached protocol documents', () => {\n   158\t        const runtime = { tools: [{ ...TOOL_A }], omitted: undefined };\n   159\t        const document = serializeCacheDocument(runtime);\n   160\t\n   161\t        expect(typeof document).toBe('string');\n   162\t        const decoded = deserializeCacheDocument(document) as { tools: Tool[] };\n   163\t        expect(decoded).toEqual({ tools: [TOOL_A] });\n   164\t        expect(decoded).not.toBe(runtime);\n   165\t        expect(decoded.tools).not.toBe(runtime.tools);\n   166\t    });\n   167\t\n   168\t    it('write skips when the captured generation moved (list_changed-during-walk guard)', async () => {\n   169\t        const store = new InMemoryResponseCacheStore();\n   170\t        const cache = new ClientResponseCache(store, false);\n   171\t        const gen = cache.captureGeneration('tools/list');\n   172\t        await cache.evict('tools/list');\n   173\t        await cache.write('tools/list', { tools: [TOOL_A] }, gen);\n   174\t        // Generation moved between capture and write → the stale aggregate is dropped.\n   175\t        expect(store.get({ method: 'tools/list', partition: PRE })).toBeUndefined();\n   176\t        // A fresh capture after the evict writes through.\n   177\t        const gen2 = cache.captureGeneration('tools/list');\n   178\t        await cache.write('tools/list', { tools: [TOOL_A] }, gen2);\n   179\t        expect(store.get({ method: 'tools/list', partition: PRE })).toBeDefined();\n   180\t    });\n    58\t\n    59\t`responseCacheStore` swaps the backing store; the default is a fresh `InMemoryResponseCacheStore` per client, holding at most 512 `resources/read` entries.\n    60\t\n    61\t```ts source=\"../../examples/guides/clients/caching.examples.ts#responseCacheStore_shared\"\n    62\tconst store = new InMemoryResponseCacheStore({ maxEntries: 2048 });\n    63\t\n    64\tconst client = new Client({ name: 'my-client', version: '1.0.0' }, { responseCacheStore: store });\n    65\t```\n    66\t\n    67\tEvery method on the `ResponseCacheStore` interface may return a promise, so a Redis-style store implements the same five methods. The client writes each entry's `value` as a serialized JSON protocol document, ready for a persistent backend; on a hit it decodes a fresh result object. Entries are keyed by connected-server identity, so one store can back many clients: connections to different servers never collide.\n    68\t\n    69\t## Partition the store per user\n    70\t\n    71\tWhen one shared store serves several principals, set `cachePartition` to a stable identity of the authorization context — the auth subject, for example.\n    72\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"agent_message","text":"Cache artık runtime nesneleri yerine JSON olarak serialize edilmiş protocol document’ları saklıyor.\n\n- Yazma/okuma codec’i ve eski nesne tabanlı custom store uyumluluğu eklendi: [responseCache.ts](/private/tmp/capsule-quality-v2/T09-codex-capsule/packages/client/src/client/responseCache.ts:250)\n- Cache hit’te her seferinde yeni decode edilmiş nesne dönüyor; `structuredClone` tamamen kaldırıldı: [client.ts](/private/tmp/capsule-quality-v2/T09-codex-capsule/packages/client/src/client/client.ts:1682)\n- `ResponseCacheStore` public imzaları korunarak depolama sözleşmesi ve dokümantasyon güncellendi.\n- Codec round-trip ve mutasyon izolasyonu testleri eklendi: [responseCache.test.ts](/private/tmp/capsule-quality-v2/T09-codex-capsule/packages/client/test/client/responseCache.test.ts:156)\n\nDoğrulamalar:\n\n- Hedef response cache + parametre aynalama: 67/67 test\n- Client test paketi: 728/728 test\n- `pnpm run lint`: geçti\n- `pnpm run typecheck`: geçti\n- `structuredClone` artık client kaynaklarında veya testlerinde bulunmuyor."}}
